---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import groups from "../data/married-in.json";

const other = groups.filter((g) => g.surname !== "Falco");
const named = other.reduce((a, g) => a + g.n, 0);
const withheld = other.reduce((a, g) => a + g.withheld, 0);
const big = other.filter((g) => g.n >= 3);
const small = other.filter((g) => g.n > 0 && g.n < 3);
const onlyLiving = other.filter((g) => g.n === 0);

// A short account for the branches big enough to have one. Everything here is
// either in the tables below or sourced elsewhere on the site; nothing new is asserted.
const STORY = {
  "Zampiello": `The rarest name in this family by a wide margin — about <strong>123 bearers in the
    world</strong>, and in the Italian records only one town: <strong>Arpaia</strong>, eight hundred
    people, four kilometres from Arienzo across the provincial border. Two Falco brothers of Forchia
    married two Zampiello sisters of Arpaia and both couples came to Brisbane; two more sisters
    followed, marrying into <strong>Servodio</strong> and <strong>Miceli</strong>. Their father
    <strong>Angelo Zampiello</strong>, born 31 August 1895, holds the family's Croce al Merito di
    Guerra for East Africa. <a href="/TheFalco/zampiello/">The Zampiello page</a>.`,
  "Crisci": `The family that married into this line <strong>twice in one generation</strong> —
    Andreana Crisci to Vincenzo Falco, and Arcangelo Crisci to Vincenzo's sister Anna. There are two
    Arcangelo Crisci alive at once, aged fifty-two and thirty in 1817, and the 1820 register settled
    which was which. They are of strada Camellara, the family's own street.`,
  "Annecchino": `A <strong>Forchia</strong> name, and the one that brought this line north out of
    Arienzo: Carminantonio Falco married <strong>Filomena Annecchino</strong>, born 10 April 1897, and
    the couple are buried side by side at Nudgee in Brisbane. Her own birth act has never been found —
    the Forchia births of 1897 are one of two volumes at Benevento that were never digitised.`,
  "Morgillo": `The second weld. Raffaele Falco of generation four married <strong>Angela Rosa
    Morgillo</strong>, born at San Felice on 24 February 1818. The Arienzo Morgillo are now two
    households — one on Monticello de' Cappuccini, one on strada Nuova di San Gaetano — and one of
    them stood beside a Falco at a town-hall desk in 1826.
    <a href="/TheFalco/morgillo/">The Morgillo page</a>.`,
  "Falcone": `<strong>Not, on this archive's reading, the same family.</strong> The Falcone of
    <strong>Capua and Caserta</strong> — Agostino, born about 1740, and his descendants through Capua,
    Galluccio and Frosinone — were once carried at the head of this line. They were removed:
    <strong>Matteo Falco of Arienzo replaces Agostino Falcone</strong>, on the evidence of the Arienzo
    baptisms. FamilySearch's index still treats Falco and Falcone as one name, which is how the
    confusion began. They are kept here because the tree keeps them, and because a reader looking for
    Falcone of Caserta should find what this archive holds and what it decided.`,
  "Mazza": `A <strong>Sicilian</strong> branch, and the only one in this archive from outside Campania
    proper. <strong>Giovanni Mazza</strong> was born at <strong>Piedimonte Etneo</strong>, on the
    eastern slope of Etna, on 28 April 1875 and died there in 1960; his son <strong>Sebastian</strong>,
    born 1920 in the same town, died at <strong>Brisbane</strong> in 2002. The family therefore crossed
    the same water as the Falco and the Zampiello, from the other end of Italy, and met them here.`,
  "Prostamo": `From <strong>Briatico</strong> in Calabria, on the Tyrrhenian coast north of Vibo
    Valentia. <strong>Domenica Prostamo</strong>, born March 1928 at Briatico, died at Brisbane in
    2010, having married into the Sicilian Mazza. One detail in this branch is worth more work than it
    has had: her mother <strong>Maria Rosa Melluso was born in NEW YORK</strong> on 14 October 1907 and
    died at Briatico in 1994 — an emigration and a return, two generations before the Australian one.`,
  "Zaino": `From <strong>Durazzano</strong>, a Benevento comune a few kilometres from Arpaia, and a
    name that also appears in the Arienzo registers. Lorenzo Zaino (1860–1945) and Costanza Abatiello
    (1871–1955) both lived and died there; their son <strong>Bartolomeo Michael Zaino</strong>, born at
    Durazzano on 20 June 1894, died at <strong>Westbury, Nassau County, New York</strong> in 1963. The
    same valley sent people to two continents.`,
  "Servodio": `<strong>Michele Servodio</strong> (1930–2021) married <strong>Raffaela Zampiello</strong>
    (1930–2021), one of the four Arpaia sisters who reached Queensland. They died at Brisbane six weeks
    apart. Their Brisbane immigration file is named in the National Archives and has never been read.`,
  "Ferrara": `Two Ferrara families sit here and this archive has not joined them. One is of
    <strong>Arpaia</strong>, where Giuseppa Ferrara was born on 29 June 1912 and where a Ferrara married
    Angelo Zampiello. The other is of <strong>San Felice a Cancello</strong>, where Giovanni Ferrara was
    born in 1848 and married a Maria Falco. Three more Ferrara lie at Nudgee in Brisbane.`,
  "Bucolo": `Seven Bucolo are buried at <strong>Nudgee Cemetery</strong> in Brisbane, and every one of
    them is in this archive only because of that index. The family tree marks its Bucolo as living; the
    graves say otherwise, which is why they appear here. Nothing yet places them in Italy.`,
  "Rivetti": `Chiara Rivetti married Pasquale Falco of generation two, and her parents are the oldest
    unanswered question in this archive: her 1843 burial calls her «filia quondam NICOLAI et ANNA», and
    <strong>two different couples of Arienzo answer to those names</strong> — one gentry, one labouring.
    Reading twenty registers has shown the Rivetti spanning the whole social range of the town, from
    bracciali to a Don, an apothecary, a priest and a nun, so the forenames settle nothing.
    <a href="/TheFalco/open-questions/">Open questions</a>.`,
};

const span = (g) => (g.first && g.last ? (g.first === g.last ? `${g.first}` : `${g.first}–${g.last}`) : "—");
---
<Base title="Married in"
      eyebrow="The families who joined this one"
      dek={`${other.length} surnames married into the Falco line. ${named} of their people are named here; ${withheld} more are alive and are not published. If one of these is your family, this page is for you.`}>

  <div class="card" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent)">
    <h3 style="margin-bottom:.4rem">Why this page exists</h3>
    <p>A pedigree keeps one child a generation and throws the rest away. Cousins go first, and the
    families who married in go with them. This page is the opposite of a pedigree: it is a list of
    <strong>every surname that joined this line</strong>, from the Crisci who married into it at
    Arienzo in the seventeen-hundreds to the Sicilian and Calabrian families who joined it in
    Brisbane in the twentieth century.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.6rem"><strong>The living are not here.</strong> That is this archive's
    standing rule and it is not bent for anyone: {withheld} people in these families are alive and
    are excluded from the public build entirely — no names, no dates, no places. What you will find
    is their dead: parents, grandparents, the ones who made the crossing. Where a family appears
    below with <em>no named people at all</em>, it means every person the archive holds for that
    surname is living.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.6rem"><strong>If this is your family and something here is wrong, it is
    wrong in public and can be fixed.</strong> Most of what is on this page comes from the family's
    own tree rather than from a record, and is marked as such throughout the archive. A date you
    know first-hand beats a date somebody typed.</p>
  </div>

  {big.map((g) => (
    <section style="margin:1.8rem 0">
      <h2 style="margin-bottom:.2rem">{g.surname}
        <span class="small mono" style="font-weight:400">{g.n} named{g.withheld ? ` · ${g.withheld} living, withheld` : ""}</span></h2>
      <p class="small" style="color:var(--ink-3);margin-bottom:.5rem">
        {span(g)}{g.places.length ? " · " + g.places.join(" · ") : ""}
        {" · "}<a href={u("/register") + "?q=" + encodeURIComponent(g.surname)}>every {g.surname} in the register →</a>
      </p>
      {STORY[g.surname] ? <p set:html={STORY[g.surname]} /> : null}
      {g.marriages.length ? (
        <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Joined by marriage:</strong>{" "}
          {g.marriages.map((m, i) => (<span>{i ? " · " : ""}{m.who} &amp; {m.to}</span>))}</p>
      ) : null}
      <div class="scroll">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Born</th><th>Died</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {g.people.map((p) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{p.name}</td>
                <td class="small mono">{[p.b, p.bp].filter(Boolean).join(" · ") || "—"}</td>
                <td class="small mono">{[p.d, p.dp].filter(Boolean).join(" · ") || "—"}</td>
              </tr>))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2 style="margin-top:2rem">One and two at a time</h2>
  <p>These surnames are in the archive but thinly — often a single wife, or a single couple, with
  nothing yet behind them. Each is a thread somebody could pull.</p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Surname</th><th>Who</th><th>When</th><th>Where</th><th></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {small.map((g) => (
          <tr>
            <td><strong>{g.surname}</strong>{g.withheld ? <><br/><span class="small" style="color:var(--ink-3)">{g.withheld} living, withheld</span></> : null}</td>
            <td>{g.people.map((p, i) => (<span>{i ? " · " : ""}{p.name}</span>))}</td>
            <td class="small mono">{span(g)}</td>
            <td class="small">{g.places.join(" · ") || "—"}</td>
            <td class="small"><a href={u("/register") + "?q=" + encodeURIComponent(g.surname)}>register →</a></td>
          </tr>))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  {onlyLiving.length ? (
    <>
      <h2 style="margin-top:2rem">Families held but not shown</h2>
      <p>For these surnames the archive holds people, and every one of them is living. Nothing is
      published: no names, no dates. They are listed here only so that a descendant searching for the
      surname can see that the family <em>is</em> in the record, and ask.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:.6rem">{onlyLiving.map((g, i) => (
        <span>{i ? " · " : ""}<strong>{g.surname}</strong> <span class="small mono">({g.withheld})</span></span>))}</p>
    </>
  ) : null}

  <h2 style="margin-top:2rem">What would help</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A death date, a town, a ship.</strong> Most of the thin entries above lack all three.</li>
    <li><strong>Photographs with names on the back.</strong> The archive already holds one portrait it
    cannot attribute — filed under two different couples in two different scans.</li>
    <li><strong>Anything from the Italian side after 1900.</strong> The registers close to the public
    at a century; the family's own papers do not.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="small">Sources for everything on this page: <a href={u("/sources")}>Sources</a> ·
  <a href={u("/register")}>The Register</a> · <a href={u("/wider-family")}>The Wider Family</a></p>
</Base>
